student_response:
I think I see what happened. I think I got a little too quick in my previous answer. I was thinking that the (3x+1) would cancel out, but I realize now that it was actually in the numerator of the fraction above it. So, I think the correct way to simplify the problem is to multiply the numerators and denominators.

Like, so:
\(
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{3 x+1}{3} \times \frac{12}{(3 x-1)(3 x+1)} \\
= & \frac{(3 x+1) \times 12}{3 \times (3 x-1) \times (3 x+1)} \\
= & \frac{12(3 x+1)}{3(3 x-1)(3 x+1)}
\end{aligned}
\)

And then the (3x+1) would cancel out, right? Leaving just:
\(
\begin{aligned}
\frac{12}{3(3 x-1)}
\end{aligned}
\)

Which simplifies to:
\(
\begin{aligned}
\frac{4}{3 x-1}
\end{aligned}
\)

So, I think the correct answer is actually D. \( \frac{4}{3 x-1} \).

